Title: Read the Pause checkbox in a live window, with a screen reader
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-16T09:38Z MinhHaDuong created

2026-09-16T10:13Z MinhHaDuong note blocker 0797 closed — Blocked-by removed.
--- body ---

## Context

Ticket 0797 replaced the dialog's on/off button with an
`<input type="checkbox">` labelled "Pause indexing". The control is built,
gated and shipped — `make check` green, mutants 91/91 and 21/21, and
`make sitter-smoke` run against a real Zotero 10 with a fresh profile.

**What no headless lane can establish is what a person hears and sees.** Three
readings are owed, all needing a human at the machine, and they are carried
here rather than left as unticked boxes on 0797 so that ticket can close on the
work it actually delivered.

That separation is the point. Ticket 0792 closed with its accessibility
criterion ticked from source, and 0797's own body records that as the defect
not to repeat. A criterion that needs a screen reader is not met by reading the
code that drives it.

## The three readings

1. **What Orca announces for the checkbox**, in each of its checked/unchecked
   and enabled/disabled combinations. The control's accessible name now comes
   from the bound `<label>` and its state from `checked`; ticket 0790's
   inverting-label defect cannot recur by construction, because a checkbox's
   name does not name an action. That is an argument, not a reading.

2. **That the native widget survives a forced-colours setting.** This is one of
   the reasons the control is a plain platform checkbox with no stylesheet and
   no ARIA role: Gecko keeps responsibility for the rendering. Unverified in a
   real window at a real high-contrast setting.

3. **That the removal notice is now spoken** — and this one is new, not a
   re-check. Until the review of PR 596 the `vanished` transition reached the
   announcer and was never read: the announcement's identity was computed from
   the phase, and a removal does not change the phase, so the kind never moved
   and the live region stayed silent. The one reader who cannot see the window
   was the one never told. The fix keys that row on `'vanished'` and was
   positive-controlled three times in the suite, but no screen reader has spoken
   it.

## Relevant files

- `verification/SDT-SITTER-UI-PANEL.md` — where the readings belong once taken;
  it already records that its own AT-SPI table and both SPEECH OUTPUT lines
  describe a control that no longer exists.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `describeSDTPauseControl`, the announcer
  node, and the `'vanished'` kind. Cite by symbol: this file moved four times
  on 2026-09-15/16.

## Test

There is no test. That is the ticket: every arm of this is a human reading, and
writing an automated assertion for it would produce exactly the false
confidence 0792 shipped. What the suite already covers — that the right string
reaches the right node, that the kind changes on removal — is green and is not
what is being asked here.

## Verification

- [ ] Orca's announcement recorded for checked/unchecked × enabled/disabled,
      verbatim, with the machine named
- [ ] The checkbox read at a forced-colours setting, with what was seen
- [ ] A removal performed with a screen reader listening, and what it said —
      including the case where it says nothing, which is the result that matters
- [ ] `verification/SDT-SITTER-UI-PANEL.md` updated with all three, replacing
      the rows that describe the old button

## Invariants

- Nothing is ticked from source. A reading absent is recorded as absent.

## Exit criteria

The three readings are in `SDT-SITTER-UI-PANEL.md`, each naming the machine and
the date, or the ticket records why one could not be taken and what would be
needed.
